# 📚 Object-Oriented Stack Design Using C++ Templates

**A generic, menu-driven stack in C++, built from a single class template.**

One `stack<type>` class handles `int`, `char`, `float`, `double` and `string` data, with push, pop and display operations and overflow/underflow detection.

[![Language](https://img.shields.io/badge/language-C++-00599C?style=for-the-badge&logo=cplusplus&logoColor=white)](https://isocpp.org/)
[![Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/status-active-16a34a?style=for-the-badge)](#)
[![PRs](https://img.shields.io/badge/PRs-welcome-4f46e5?style=for-the-badge)](#)

---

## ✨ Features

| Feature | Description |
| --- | --- |
| 🧩 **Generic stack** | One `template<class type>` class reused for every data type |
| ➕ **Push** | Adds an element on top; reports `stack overflow` when full |
| ➖ **Pop** | Removes the top element; reports `stack underflow` when empty |
| 👀 **Display** | Prints all elements from bottom to top, or `stack is empty` |
| 🗂️ **Five data types** | `int`, `char`, `float`, `double`, `string` |
| 🔁 **New or previous stack** | Start fresh or continue with the stack as you left it |
| 🖥️ **Console menu** | Interactive, menu-driven interface |

---

## 🏗️ Architecture

`main()` shows the type menu and calls one handler function per data type. Each handler runs its own push/pop/display menu on a global `stack<type>` object, so the stack keeps its contents when you leave and re-enter.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    user(("User")) --> main["main()<br/>type menu"]

    main --> int_stack["int_stack()"]
    main --> char_stack["char_stack()"]
    main --> float_stack["float_stack()"]
    main --> double_stack["double_stack()"]
    main --> string_stack["string_stack()"]

    int_stack --> si["stack&lt;int&gt;"]
    char_stack --> sc["stack&lt;char&gt;"]
    float_stack --> sf["stack&lt;float&gt;"]
    double_stack --> sd["stack&lt;double&gt;"]
    string_stack --> ss["stack&lt;string&gt;"]

    si & sc & sf & sd & ss --> ops["push · pop · display"]

    classDef entry fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#2563eb,color:#172554
    classDef handler fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#d97706,color:#78350f
    classDef tmpl fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#16a34a,color:#14532d
    class user,main entry
    class int_stack,char_stack,float_stack,double_stack,string_stack handler
    class si,sc,sf,sd,ss,ops tmpl
```

### The class template

```cpp
#define MAX_SIZE 5

template<class type>
class stack
{
    type array[MAX_SIZE];   // fixed-size storage
    int TOP;                // index of the top element, -1 when empty
public:
    stack();                // sets TOP = -1
    void push(type x);      // "stack overflow" when full
    void pop();             // "stack underflow" when empty
    void display();         // "stack is empty" or the elements
};
```

| State | Condition |
| --- | --- |
| Empty | `TOP == -1` |
| Full | `TOP == 4` (capacity of 5) |

---

## 🔄 How It Works

1. **Pick a type** from the main menu (1 to 5), or `6` to exit.
2. **Choose** a new stack (`1`) or continue with the previous one (`2`).
3. **Operate** on the stack with push, pop and display until you choose exit.
4. **Return** to the main menu and switch to another type. Each type keeps its own stack.

